# CODEOWNERS — the chokepoint files
#
# Purpose: several files in this repository are touched by almost EVERY feature.
# With multiple workers (human and agent) on parallel branches, those files are where
# merges collide, and a bad automated resolution in one of them breaks the client, the
# generated types and the API reference at the same time.
#
# Listing them here makes GitHub request a review automatically whenever they change.
# That is advisory unless branch protection also requires code-owner review — see
# docs/PARALLEL-WORK.md for why that is deliberately NOT switched on yet.

# ── The contract: one 7,000+ line object literal that every new endpoint edits ──
# Everything downstream is generated from it — the RTK Query client, the request and
# response types, docs/API.md, and the cache invalidation map. A conflict resolved
# carelessly here is a build that fails in three places at once.
/captain-yard-web/src/lib/contract.ts          @subinsayzz
/captain-yard-web/src/lib/contract-routes.ts   @subinsayzz

# ── The document model ──
# Every new field lands here. Fields cluster by domain more naturally than the contract
# does, so collisions are rarer — but a wrong merge silently changes what a document is.
/captain-yard-web/src/server/db/schema.ts      @subinsayzz

# ── Tenant isolation ──
# The scoped repository is the only thing standing between one account's data and
# another's. isolation.test.ts greps the tree to keep it that way. Any change here is a
# change to the security model, whatever it looks like in the diff.
/captain-yard-web/src/server/db/repo.ts        @subinsayzz
/captain-yard-web/src/server/db/indexes.ts     @subinsayzz
/captain-yard-web/src/server/auth/             @subinsayzz

# ── The pure domain engine ──
# No IO, no dependencies, and a purity test that fails the build if that changes.
/captain-yard-web/packages/engine/             @subinsayzz

# ── The gates themselves ──
/.github/                                      @subinsayzz
